#!/bin/bash
# imac-audio-module - RETIRED. Superseded by scripts/imac-audio.
#
# This script builds davidjo's driver as a hand-placed kernel module under
# updates/ext01, patching the kernel source tree directly. That is a coherent
# approach, but it is not the configuration this machine runs, and the script
# mis-states which driver is correct. Two of its claims were wrong:
#
#   1. It says the loaded davidjo DKMS package "IS the working configuration"
#      and advises:  sudo dkms remove snd_hda_macbookpro/0.2 --all
#      That removes the headset capture fix. jackdanyell's tree is the one that
#      carries it.
#
#   2. It looks for patch_cs8409.c.diff with a patch_cirrus/ header set and
#      builds against the kernel source in /usr/src. The working build goes
#      through DKMS instead, where the upstream install.cirrus.driver.sh runs
#      as PRE_BUILD and does the patching.
#
# Its own comment block records that this disagreement was known and
# unresolved, and resolved it wrongly.
#
# Kept as a stub so an older guide fails with an explanation. History is in
# git; the original is at commit 4c7b902.

cat >&2 <<'EOF'

  imac-audio-module: RETIRED - do not run this.

  It builds a different driver by a different method, and it advises removing
  the one that actually works.

  Use instead:

      sudo imac-audio --check     verify what is loaded now
      sudo imac-audio --install   build and install the patched driver
      sudo imac-audio --mic       microphone diagnostics
      imac-audio --help           full options

  See docs/audio.md for why the old conclusion was wrong.

EOF

exit 1